    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/3613702": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor",
      "description": "Adjunct Professor. University of St. Thomas. Adjunct Professor Position Overview The University of St. Thomas (UST), a private Catholic university in Houston, Texas, invites applications for Adjunct Professors. This part-time teaching position involves providing undergraduate and/or graduate instruction in the appointed discipline, supporting the university's mission of academic excellence rooted in Catholic traditions. Key Responsibilities Teaching: Deliver high-quality instruction in assigned courses, adhering to the university's academic standards and integrating Catholic intellectual traditions where appropriate. Course Development: Prepare and update course materials, including syllabi, lectures, assignments, and assessments, ensuring alignment with departmental objectives. Student Engagement: Maintain regular office hours to support and mentor students, fostering an inclusive and supportive learning environment. Assessment: Evaluate student performance through assignments, examinations, and other assessment tools, providing timely and constructive feedback. Professional Development: Stay informed about advancements in the relevant field and incorporate new knowledge into teaching practices. University Service: Collaborate with departmental faculty and contribute to the academic community as appropriate for adjunct faculty. Qualifications Educational Background: A minimum of a Master's degree in the relevant field; a terminal degree is preferred. Teaching Experience: Prior experience teaching at the college or university level is preferred. Communication Skills: Excellent verbal and written communication abilities. Commitment to Mission: Willingness to support and promote the Catholic mission and values of the University of St. Thomas. Technological Proficiency: Familiarity with instructional technologies and online learning platforms is advantageous. Application Process Interested candidates should submit the following materials: A current curriculum vitae (CV). A cover letter detailing teaching experience, areas of expertise, and alignment with UST's mission. Contact information for previous employers The University of St. Thomas is an Equal Opportunity Employer."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/3085629": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor - Clinical Mental Health Counseling",
      "description": "Adjunct Professor - Clinical Mental Health Counseling. University of St. Thomas. University of St. Thomas - Houston School of Education and Human Services Adjunct Professor - Clinical Mental Health Counseling The University of St. Thomas (UST), a private Catholic University committed to the liberal arts and the religious and intellectual tradition of Catholic higher education, located in Houston, Texas, near the vibrant central business district, Texas Medical Center, and museum district, is seeking applicants for an adjunct faculty position to teach various graduate clinical mental health counseling courses in the CACREP-accredited department. In addition to core counseling classes, the department is specifically looking for faculty with expertise in research and program evaluation coursework. SUMMARY: Provide the expertise and knowledge that support the graduate clinical mental health counseling curriculum. All clinical mental health classes are offered in the daytime on Mondays and Wednesdays at the Houston campus. The qualifications listed below are representative of the education, experience, knowledge, skills and/or abilities required. EDUCATION: A Ph.D. or Ed.D. in Counselor Education and Supervision or related field is required. EXPERIENCE: Teaching experience at the graduate level in counseling or related fields; ability to teach in a face-to-face format and demonstrated excellence in teaching. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Must have mastery of assigned course content. Must provide teaching evaluations with include quantitative and qualitative feedback. Must be able to teach in-person on the Houston campus. Must be familiar with Blackboard and Office 365. Must have excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to communicate effectively with a diverse student population. Must possess good organizational and planning skills. Must have experience with students from diverse academic, socio-economic, cultural and ethnic backgrounds and students with disabilities. Must demonstrate ability to inspire and motivate students in a learning-centered environment. To apply for this position, please submit a cover letter addressing qualifications for the position; curriculum vitae; philosophy of teaching; official transcripts, two professional references, and a statement concerning alignment to the mission of Catholic higher education in light of Ex corde ecclesiae. The University of St. Thomas is an Equal Opportunity Employer"
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/3557501": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor for John Paul II Institute",
      "description": "Adjunct Professor for John Paul II Institute. University of St. Thomas. Adjunct Professor for John Paul II Institute University of St. Thomas - Houston, TX Theology The University of St. Thomas Houston Graduate Program for the \"Master of Arts in John Paul II Studies\" is looking for an adjunct professor of Theology to teach the course JPII 5308 Vatican II and Sources of Renewal . This course examines the event, documents, and reception of Vatican Council II as evident through the participation and writings of Cardinal Karol Wojtyla, beginning in his earlier work Sources of Renewal and various later writings as Pope John Paul II. We then study the sources of renewal in the Eucharist, studying especially the documents: Ecclesia de Eucharistia (\"The Church of the Eucharist\") and Redemptoris Mater (\"Mother of the Redeemer\"). The successful candidate will have a Ph.D. in Theology or Philosophy or Medieval Studies or the equivalent with the relevant scholarly and teaching experience. The University of St. Thomas is an Equal Opportunity Employer."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/3602231": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor in Global Cybersecurity",
      "description": "Adjunct Professor in Global Cybersecurity. University of St. Thomas. Adjunct Professor in Global Cybersecurity The University of St. Thomas ( www.stthom.edu ), a private Catholic university located in Houston, Texas, the nation's fourth largest city, committed to the liberal arts and the religious and intellectual tradition of Catholic higher education, invites applications from outstanding candidates for multiple part-time positions of Adjunct Professor of Global Cybersecurity housed in the Department of International Studies & Modern Languages. The Department of International Studies & Modern Languages at the University of St. Thomas is a multidisciplinary program providing quality education to the next generation of global leaders. The Department consists of six full-time faculty and offers four major programs, including International Studies, International Development, Global Cybersecurity, and Spanish, along with East Asia Studies and Latin American Studies minors and its own graduate program, Master in Diplomacy & Strategic Affairs. We seek candidates with teaching and research interests in cybersecurity policy and practices. Preferences will be given to candidates who have extensive experiences in the field. The successful candidate must hold a Master's degree in Cybersecurity or related discipline. A doctoral degree is preferred. Interested candidates must provide the following: 1) a complete Curriculum Vitae; 2) two letters of reference. In order to ensure a harmonious and fruitful relationship between new faculty and the university, applicants are encouraged to consider carefully the university's understanding of its Catholic mission as described here: https://www.stthom.edu/Offices-and-Services/Human- Resources/Index.aqf [scroll down to \"Faculty Employment\" - Learn More] The start date for the position is Spring 2025. Candidate must have degree in hand by the start date. Review of applications will begin on immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position can be addressed to Yao-Yuan Yeh, Chair of the International Studies & Modern Languages Department, yehy@stthom.edu . The University of St. Thomas is an Equal Opportunity Employer"
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/3646251": {
      "title": "Adjunct Professor- School of Education",
      "description": "Adjunct Professor- School of Education. University of St. Thomas. The University of St. Thomas (UST), a private Catholic University committed to the liberal arts and the religious and intellectual tradition of Catholic higher education, located in Houston, Texas, near the vibrant central business district, Texas Medical Center, and museum district, is seeking applicants for an adjunct faculty position to teach various undergraduate courses in the department. Any and all faculty hired through this position will also work closely with the Associates of Applied Science in Pragmatic Studies program in Kolbe School of Innovation and Professional Studies, and adjuncts will be encouraged to teach in both programs. SUMMARY: Provide the expertise and knowledge that support the BAAS Social Innovation & Human Service curriculum. Classes are only offered as in-person format and will take place on UST campus during daytimes and evenings in Houston, TX. The qualifications listed below are representative of the education, experience, knowledge, skills and/or abilities required. EDUCATION: A Master's degree is required with a minimum of 18-credit hours (graduate) in education and all related fields, especially special education/exceptionality, criminology/law, business, psychology, sociology, computers/IT, health, political science, history, statistics and research, communications, and human services. Special attention is given to applicants with experience in working with students who have learning differences. EXPERIENCE: Teaching experience at any level, specifically with students who have learning differences. Must also have the ability to teach in a face-to-face format and have demonstrated excellence in teaching. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: • Must have knowledge and training related to working with students who have learning differences (state teaching certification is not required) • Must be able to teach in-person on the Houston campus. • Must be familiar with Office 365. • Must have excellent interpersonal skills and the ability to communicate effectively with a diverse student population. • Must possess good organizational and planning skills. • Must have experience with, or be willing to obtain training in, Universal Design for Learning (UDL). • Must demonstrate ability to inspire and motivate students in a learning-centered environment. To apply for this position, please submit a cover letter addressing qualifications for the position; curriculum vitae; philosophy of teaching; official transcripts, two professional references, and a statement concerning alignment to the mission of Catholic higher education in light of Ex corde ecclesiae. The University of St. Thomas is an Equal Opportunity Employer"
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4192126": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - International Studies (Latin American Studies)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - International Studies (Latin American Studies). University of St. Thomas. Assistant Professor of International Studies (Latin American Studies) The University of St. Thomas (www.stthom.edu), a private Catholic university located in Houston, Texas, the nation's fourth-largest city, committed to the liberal arts and the religious and intellectual tradition of Catholic higher education, invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position as Assistant Professor of International Studies (Latin American Studies) housed in the Department of Political Science, International Studies, and Modern Languages. The Department of Political Science, International Studies, and Modern Languages at the University of St. Thomas is a multidisciplinary program providing quality education to the next generation of global leaders. The Department consists of ten full-time faculty members and offers eight undergraduate programs, including Political Science, Pre-Law, International Studies, Global Cybersecurity, Diplomacy, East Asia Studies, Latin American Studies, and Spanish, as well as its own graduate programs, the Master's in Diplomacy & Strategic Affairs (MDSA) and Master's in Public Policy and Public Administration (MPPA). We seek candidates with teaching and research expertise in Latin American studies, broadly defined, with disciplinary backgrounds in the social sciences or humanities. The ability to contribute to Spanish-language instruction is preferred but not required. The successful candidate will strengthen the Department's curricular and scholarly coverage of Latin America while contributing to its interdisciplinary undergraduate and graduate programs. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in a relevant social science or humanities field by the time of appointment and demonstrate teaching experience in related areas. Evidence of sustained academic, professional, research, or field engagement with Latin America is highly desirable. Teaching responsibilities may include courses in the candidate's area of specialization as well as broader courses in Latin American Studies, International Studies, and, where qualified, Spanish language. Interested candidates must provide the following: 1) a cover letter describing your teaching, scholarship, mentorship, and service, detailing any connections to the mission of the University of St. Thomas; 2) a complete Curriculum Vitae; 3) a statement demonstrating willingness to support the tenets of Catholic higher education as articulated in Ex Corde Ecclesiae; 4) a statement on your teaching philosophy; 5) teaching evaluations (if available); 6) three letters of reference. The start date for the position is Fall 2027. The candidate must have a degree in hand by the start date. Application review will begin on October 31, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Please direct questions about the position to Yao-Yuan Yeh, Chair, Department of Political Science, International Studies, and Modern Languages, at yehy@stthom.edu . The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/3761275": {
      "title": "Athletics Assistant Coach- Stipend Only",
      "description": "Athletics Assistant Coach- Stipend Only. University of St. Thomas. Athletics Assistant Coach- Stipend UST is seeking committed and enthusiastic part-time (stipend) Coaches to support our collegiate athletic programs. These positions are ideal for individuals looking to gain coaching experience, contribute to student-athlete development, and be a part of a competitive and collaborative team environment. The application process for stipend coaches is not a formal job application; however, UST screens candidates (criminal background and motor vehicle records search) to ensure participants meet University requirements."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/3751759": {
      "title": "Athletics Volunteer",
      "description": "Athletics Volunteer. University of St. Thomas. UST is seeking committed and enthusiastic Volunteer Coaches to support our collegiate athletic programs. This is an unpaid opportunity ideal for individuals looking to gain coaching experience, contribute to student-athlete development, and be a part of a competitive and collaborative team environment. The application process for Volunteers is not a formal job application; however, UST screens candidates (criminal background and motor vehicle records search) to ensure participants meet University requirements."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4198518": {
      "title": "Campus Minister",
      "description": "Campus Minister. University of St. Thomas. University of St. Thomas Campus Minister Campus Ministry The University of St. Thomas (UST), a private Catholic University committed to the liberal arts and the religious and intellectual tradition of Catholic higher education, located in Houston, Texas, near the vibrant central business district, Texas Medical Center, and museum district, is seeking applicants for a full-time Campus Minister. The position reports directly to the Director of Campus Ministry and is within the Student Affairs organization. This position will actively contribute towards furthering our Student Affairs Board of Trustee charges to strengthen UST's (a) student success efforts and (b) Catholic mission and faith integration efforts. Applicants for this position must be Catholic, and they need to be able to support joyfully, professionally, and publicly the University as it seeks to be faithful to and embody both a robust Catholicism--in its intellectual, doctrinal, moral, and spiritual dimensions. Position Summary: The Campus Minister is a ministry position directly involved with student formation in the areas of Catholic outreach and spiritual integration of the campus community. This position develops and implements retreats, service opportunities, and peer ministry initiatives, as well as provides support for students leading these initiatives, acts as a liaison with St. Paul's Outreach (SPO) missionaries, and forms small group leaders and faith-centered living communities known as \"households.\" The Campus Minister has the authority to implement operating practices, carries out major assignments in conducting the operations of the organization, and is involved in planning long-term and short-term organizational objectives. This position reports directly to the Director of Campus Ministry. Essential Duties and Responsibilities: Provide pastoral care and spiritual guidance to students on an ongoing basis Inspire, form, and equip students, faculty, and staff so that they would grow in holiness, form a deeper relationship with God, and invite others to join them by meeting them where they are, building Christ-centered relationships with them, and forming them into lifelong disciples. Form, equip, and send Peer Ministers to evangelize the UST community with ongoing meetings and one-on-ones, as well as lead them in organizing, planning, and running student outreach events. Plan, guide, and lead retreats, alongside the Retreats Intern, to provide overnight and daily faith experiences for students on and off campus, such as the Fall Retreat, Men's and Women's Retreats, Packing Nothing Retreat, Athlete Retreat, Household Retreat, Leadership Retreats, etc. Guide and supervise Households to be Christ-centered living; plan and lead household-related events. Invite, form, and send small group leaders to lead intentional small groups on campus, and continue to shepherd/mentor them. Mentor student leaders on campus Organize and lead the UST group (students and staff) that will attend the annual SEEK Conference, including coordination of all trip logistics and formation of students. Work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and on-campus groups to engage students in Campus Ministry activities. Work alongside other Campus Ministry staff in planning and executing all student leadership-related activities, such as meetings, retreats, and training. Advise the Campus Ministry team on various aspects of Catholic identity for the Participate in various meetings that help support Campus Ministry goals and Serve as UST representative on Young Adult and Campus Ministry activities with the Archdiocese of Galveston-Houston. May serve as a representative of Campus Ministry, as requested (e.g. Care Team, Spiritual Integration Committee, etc.) Act as liaison between Campus Ministry and Paul's Outreach missionaries, helping to organize formation activities and outreach events with the missionaries. Perform other duties as assigned by the Director of Campus Complete all administrative office work, including maintaining the budget and proper communication with co-workers. Experience and Education Requirements: Bachelor's degree required; preference will be given to candidates with associated degrees in Theology, Religious Studies, Catechetics, or similar fields of Other areas of study are acceptable with additional years of ministry experience. Preference for a Master's degree in Divinity or Theology from an accredited Catholic institution Must be a practicing Catholic in good standing with the Roman Catholic Minimum 2 years of experience in ministry-related activities that concern the faith development of youth or young adults. Excellent communication skills, both verbal and Fluency in reading, writing, and speaking Spanish is strongly Excellent organization skills and the ability to prioritize projects and Ability to work effectively in collaboration with Possess the enthusiasm, creativity, knowledge, and energy needed to meet the demands of the position. Demonstrate an understanding of the spiritual and pastoral needs of the targeted age group which they intend to serve. Must be reliable and dependable to meet the scheduled work hours, which will include evenings and weekends, as planned. Demonstrate an intermediate level of proficiency in Microsoft Office suite and other graphic design software (e.g., Canva, InDesign, Finale, etc.) Working Conditions Position will require work during non-traditional business hours and on Sundays, Holy Days (e.g., Easter, Christmas, etc.), and weekends at specific events such as service projects, retreats, evening programs, student conferences, etc. Physical requirements are those of a normal business office. A valid driver's license is required in order to attend retreats and other events off-campus. The above statements are intended to describe the general nature of work performed by individuals in this job classification. They are not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of all responsibilities, duties, and qualifications required of individuals in this job classification. All positions may have miscellaneous job duties assigned on an as-needed basis. Management, at the appropriate level, retains the right to assign and change the duties of any position at any time. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4233976": {
      "title": "Controller",
      "description": "Controller. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS CONTROLLER BUSINESS OFFICE Position Summary The Controller provides leadership and oversight for the University's accounting operations and financial reporting. Reporting to the Chief Financial Officer, this position is responsible for the integrity of the general ledger, timely and accurate financial statements, effective internal controls, audit coordination, regulatory compliance, and development of a strong, service-oriented accounting team. The Controller serves as a key financial partner to University leadership and works across departments to strengthen processes, accountability, and stewardship of institutional resources. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Lead accounting operations, including the general ledger, accounts payable, accounts receivable, cash accounting, fixed assets, restricted funds, endowments, grants, and related reconciliations. Direct monthly, quarterly, and fiscal year-end close processes and ensure deadlines are met with complete, accurate, and well-supported balances. Prepare and review internal and external financial statements, management reports, account analyses, and other financial information for the CFO and University leadership. Maintain the integrity of the chart of accounts and ensure consistent application of accounting policies, fund accounting principles, and institutional procedures. Coordinate the annual financial statement audit, benefit plan audits, tax filings, and other required reviews; serve as the primary liaison with external auditors for accounting matters. Establish, document, monitor, and improve internal controls to protect University assets and support accurate, compliant financial reporting. Oversee accounting for restricted gifts, grants, endowments, capital projects, and other designated funds, including appropriate releases and reporting. Partner with Financial Planning and Analysis on budget-to-actual reporting, forecasts, financial analysis, and resolution of significant variances. Provide accounting guidance to campus departments and promote consistent, timely, and transparent financial practices. Lead, supervise, develop, and evaluate accounting staff; establish clear expectations, cross-training, documentation, and appropriate segregation of duties. Support treasury, taxation, insurance management, cash management, debt, banking, insurance, compliance, and related financial matters as assigned by the CFO. Prepare or support materials for executive leadership, the Finance Committee, Audit Committee, and Board of Directors, as requested. Perform other duties and special projects consistent with the responsibilities of the position. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, or a closely related field. Minimum of twelve years of progressively responsible accounting experience, including experience leading accounting operations and staff and non-profit account experience Strong knowledge of U.S. GAAP, financial reporting, account reconciliations, audit requirements, and internal controls, as well as non-profit accounting standards. Demonstrated experience managing month-end and year-end close processes and producing timely, accurate financial statements. Advanced Excel skills and the ability to analyze complex financial information, identify issues, and communicate practical solutions. Ability to work collaboratively with executive leadership, auditors, department leaders, and staff while maintaining accountability and confidentiality. Preferred Qualifications PeopleSoft Financials experience is highly preferred. Certified Public Accountant (CPA) designation preferred. Experience in higher education, nonprofit accounting, or another complex fund-accounting environment preferred. Experience with grants, restricted funds, endowments, debt, capital projects, and tax-exempt organizations preferred. Experience improving financial systems, close processes, reporting, and internal controls. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Strong leadership, judgment, organization, and follow-through. Clear, professional communication with the ability to explain financial matters to financial and nonfinancial audiences. Ability to balance strategic priorities with hands-on review of reconciliations, schedules, transactions, and supporting documentation. Commitment to accuracy, service, collaboration, continuous improvement, and responsible stewardship. Ability to manage competing deadlines and maintain high standards in a fast-paced environment. Working Conditions This position operates primarily in a professional office environment and requires regular use of standard office and financial systems. Occasional extended hours may be required during close periods, audits, budget cycles, and other critical deadlines. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals to perform the essential functions of the position. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4231750": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Student Success & Retention",
      "description": "Coordinator, Student Success & Retention. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS COORDINATOR, STUDENT SUCCESS & RETENTION DIVISION OF STUDENT SUCCESS Position Summary The Division of Student Success at the University of St. Thomas seeks a collaborative, student-centered, and strategic professional to serve as Coordinator of Student Success & Retention. This position is a dual-function role - equal parts student-facing and data-facing. This person provides direct success coaching to Celts flagged through Watermark SS&E early alerts or EdSights campaigns who do not have an assigned coach, ensuring no alerted student goes unserved. Simultaneously, this person owns the coaching data tracking layer across the Division, surfacing a weekly picture of who is being reached, who is not, and why - so the Executive Director can make timely, informed decisions. Key Responsibilities Direct Success Coaching Coach students flagged via Watermark early alerts or EdSights who have no assigned success coach - contact within 48 hours of every alert; same-day call for high-risk flags. Conduct needs assessments using the Division's four-pillar model: Motivation, Belonging, Academic Preparedness, and Emotional Well-Being. Connect students to resources - Knack tutoring, Celt Food Pantry, Financial Aid, Counseling, Dreams.US. Log all contacts in Watermark SS&E within 24 hours; book next appointment before the student leaves. Serve as a full member of the coaching team; escalate non-responsive students to the Retention Task Force after three failed outreach attempts. Coaching Data Tracking Conduct a weekly Monday data pull across Watermark SS&E, EdSights, Knack Tutoring, and the Who's Next walk-in kiosk - identifying unresolved alerts, unlogged contacts, and coverage gaps. Cross-reference platforms to surface students falling through the cracks: visited Who's Next but never booked a session; responded to EdSights but were never followed up with. Identify population-level patterns - which courses, majors, or student groups generate disproportionate alerts - and flag structural issues to the Executive Director. Prepare a monthly coaching coverage report: unique students coached, alert response rates, caseload distribution, session note compliance, and EdSights-to-Watermark conversion rate. Qualifications Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Higher Education, Psychology, Counseling, Social Work, Education, or a related field. 1–3 years in higher education student services, coaching, advising, or case management. Demonstrated ability to build trust quickly with students in distress, as a meaningful first connection is often required within 48 hours of a crisis flag. Proficiency in data entry, data tracking, and identifying the broader patterns that data reveals. This individual must be as comfortable pulling weekly platform reports and spotting trends as they are sitting across from a student during an initial coaching session. Organized and deadline-driven, with the ability to consistently complete the Monday data pull, a non-negotiable weekly commitment on which the entire coaching team depends. Experience with student success platforms, such as Watermark SS&E, EdSights, or equivalent systems, or a demonstrated ability to learn new technologies quickly. Commitment to the Catholic and Basilian mission of UST and to the holistic development and success of every Celt. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in Higher Education, Counseling, Social Work, Student Affairs, or a related field. Experience with Watermark SS&E, EAB Navigate, or a comparable early alert and academic advising platform. Experience with EdSights or a similar text-based student outreach, engagement, and analytics platform. Experience developing, maintaining, or managing data-tracking and reporting systems for student services or student success teams. Experience working at a first-generation-serving institution, Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI), or other mission-driven college or university. Bilingual proficiency in English and Spanish, strongly preferred given UST's student population and the level of direct student contact required in this role. Familiarity with FERPA regulations and best practices related to student privacy, records management, and appropriate data disclosure. Work Environment and Schedule This is a student-facing position that follows the University's standard working hours requirements. Regular availability and presence during established office hours are essential to providing reliable support to students, faculty, staff and campus partners. Employees receive vacation time for rest, recuperation, and personal matters. Because of the nature and scope of this position, vacation scheduling should be coordinated to minimize conflicts with important meetings, events, scheduled office activities, and critical operational deadlines. How to Apply UST invites applications from candidates committed to student success, the Catholic intellectual tradition, and the holistic development of every Celt. We especially welcome candidates with lived experience with first-generation, Hispanic, or underserved student populations. Please submit the following as a single PDF: Cover Letter addressing your coaching and/or case management experience, experience with data tracking and analysis, and commitment to the University of St. Thomas mission (maximum two pages). Current Resume or Curriculum Vitae (CV). Three Professional References. Mission and Values The University of St. Thomas is a private Catholic liberal arts university in Houston, Texas, guided by the Basilian values of Goodness, Discipline, and Knowledge. UST serves approximately 3,200 students and holds Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) designation. A significant portion of our students are first-generation college students - and the Division of Student Success coordinates the institutional response to ensure every Celt has a clear pathway to graduate, flourish, and live out their calling. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4229202": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Military Engagement and Operations",
      "description": "Coordinator, Military Engagement and Operations. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS COORDINATOR, MILITARY ENGAGEMENT AND OPERATIONS VETERAN SUCCESS CENTER We are the University of St. Thomas, the Catholic University in the heart of Houston. We are committed to the Catholic intellectual tradition and the dialogue between faith and reason. By pursuing excellence in teaching, scholarship, and service, we embody and instill in our students the core values of our founders, the Basilian Fathers: goodness, discipline, and knowledge. We foster engagement in a diverse, collaborative community. The University of St. Thomas is a comprehensive university, grounded in the liberal arts. Committed to the unity of all knowledge, we offer programs in the traditional liberal arts, professional, and skill-based disciplines. Graduates of the University of St. Thomas think critically, communicate effectively, succeed professionally, and lead ethically. A resume and cover letter must be submitted to be considered for this position. JOB SUMMARY: The Military & Veteran Engagement Coordinator supports student engagement and day-to-day operations across Student Success, Veteran Services, and Military Relations. The Coordinator is primarily responsible for developing engaging digital content, promoting programs and events to students and the military-connected community, and managing departmental budgets and financial processes. Working in collaboration with University Marketing and departmental leadership, the Coordinator helps maintain a strong and consistent connection with students through social media, digital outreach, event promotion, and storytelling. The position also provides essential financial and operational support by monitoring budgets, tracking expenditures, processing purchases, and maintaining accurate financial records. This is a 30-hour-per-week position supporting the Veteran Success Center and Celt Success Center. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Student & Digital Engagement: Develop engaging digital and social media content highlighting students, programs, resources, events, accomplishments, and military and veteran community partnerships. Coordinate social media engagement in collaboration with University Marketing and in accordance with University branding and communication standards. Develop and maintain a content and engagement calendar to ensure consistent and timely outreach. Promote programs, events, deadlines, and resources to students through coordinated email, text, social media, and established campus communication channels. Create student-facing graphics, flyers, digital content, event promotions, and other engagement materials. Capture photos, video, and short-form content at programs and events to showcase student experiences and departmental impact. Identify creative opportunities to increase student awareness, participation, and engagement with Student Success, Veteran Services, and Military Relations. Budget & Financial Operations Monitor and maintain departmental budgets for Student Success, Veteran Services, and Military Relations. Track expenditures and maintain accurate and current budget records. Process purchase requests, invoices, reimbursements, and other departmental financial transactions. Reconcile expenses and provide routine budget updates to departmental leadership. Coordinate purchasing for programs, events, promotional materials, supplies, and other departmental needs. Assist with annual budget preparation, projections, and financial reporting. Program & Event Support Coordinate promotional timelines and student outreach for departmental programs and events. Assist with event logistics, including reservations, purchasing, catering, registration, materials, and day-of-event support. Maintain departmental event and engagement calendars. Support initiatives designed to engage current students, prospective military-connected students, veterans, families, and community partners. Provide operational support for major Student Success, Veteran Services, and Military Relations programs and initiatives. Department Operations Provide operational support to the Executive Director of Student Success, Director of Veteran Services, and Director of Military Relations. Maintain organized financial, program, and engagement records. Assist with special projects as needed. Serve as a welcoming and professional point of contact for students, faculty, staff, veterans, military-connected families, and community partners. Perform other related duties as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Associate degree or equivalent combination of education and relevant professional experience. Minimum of two (2) years of experience in administrative operations, budget support, social media/digital engagement, event coordination, higher education, or a related area. Demonstrated experience creating engaging content for social media or other digital platforms. Experience tracking budgets, expenses, purchasing, or other financial transactions. Strong written and verbal communication skills with the ability to create clear, engaging, and professional student-facing content. Strong organizational skills and demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities, deadlines, and projects simultaneously. Proficiency with Microsoft Office, including Outlook, Word, Excel, and PowerPoint. Ability to work independently, exercise sound judgment, and maintain a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. Ability to work effectively and professionally with students, faculty, staff, veterans, military-connected individuals, and community partners. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor's degree. Experience working in higher education, military/veteran services, student affairs, student success, or a related environment. Prior military service or demonstrated experience working with veterans or military-connected communities. Experience creating content using Canva or similar graphic design platforms. Experience developing short-form video, photography, social media, or other digital content. Experience with budget reconciliation, purchasing, procurement, or financial reporting. Experience supporting events, programs, or student engagement initiatives. POSITION REQUIREMENTS Ability to work a consistent 30-hour-per-week schedule, with occasional evening or weekend hours to support programs and events. Ability to attend campus events and capture digital content as needed. Ability to manage confidential student, departmental, and financial information with discretion. Must demonstrate reliability, initiative, creativity, attention to detail, and a strong commitment to student service. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4231753": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Student Success Programs & Strategic Outreach",
      "description": "Coordinator, Student Success Programs & Strategic Outreach. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS COORDINATOR OF STUDENT SUCCESS PROGRAMS & STRATEGIC OUTREACH DIVISION OF STUDENT SUCCESS Position Summary The Division of Student Success at the University of St. Thomas seeks a collaborative, student-centered, and strategic professional to serve as Coordinator of Student Success Programs & Strategic Outreach. The Coordinator of Student Success Programs & Strategic Outreach leads student engagement initiatives that promote connection, belonging, and academic success. This highly student-facing role plans and facilitates programs and events, builds relationships with students across the university, and supports strategic outreach efforts through effective communication, follow-up, and data tracking. The ideal candidate is passionate about working with students, creating welcoming and inclusive environments, and using data to inform outreach and improve student outcomes. Individuals with experience supporting first-generation, Hispanic, and other historically underserved student populations are strongly encouraged to apply. The role also carries direct planning responsibility for Mendenhall Summer Institute and Freshman Symposium - two of UST's signature student success programs - and serves as back-up success coach for students who receive an academic alert but have no assigned success coach. This person works across every program in the Division. Key Responsibilities Plan, coordinate, and implement student engagement programs and events that foster student connection, belonging, and success. Collaborate with campus departments and student-facing offices to develop and promote cross-functional programming and outreach initiatives. Build and maintain relationships with students through direct outreach, event participation, and follow-up communication. Coordinate logistics, communications, scheduling, participant tracking, and assessment activities for student success programs and special initiatives. Serve as co-planner and primary administrative coordinator for the Mendenhall Summer Institute, including student communications, event logistics, and program implementation. Act as a primary student-facing representative for the Mendenhall Summer Institute, providing guidance and support before, during, and after the program. Support the planning and execution of Freshman Symposium, including participant communications, scheduling, event coordination, stipend processing, and administrative support. Maintain accurate and comprehensive records of student interactions, outreach efforts, and engagement activities across multiple student success platforms. Reconcile student contact data across systems to ensure consistency, completeness, and compliance with institutional standards. Monitor student engagement trends and generate reports to support assessment, planning, and decision-making. Provide outreach and intervention support for students who have been identified through academic, attendance, behavioral, wellness, or engagement alerts when no assigned Success Coach is available. Conduct timely follow-up through phone calls, emails, text messaging, virtual meetings, and in-person appointments. Connect students with appropriate campus resources and services to address academic, personal, financial, and well-being concerns. Document student contacts, interventions, referrals, and outcomes within designated student success systems. Identify and escalate high-risk student concerns to appropriate campus offices and support teams. Support retention, persistence, and student success initiatives through proactive outreach, communication campaigns, and coordinated intervention efforts. Perform other duties as assigned in support of student success, engagement, retention, and strategic outreach objectives. Knowledge of key platforms EdSights and Watermark. Qualifications Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Higher Education, Student Affairs, Education, Communication, or related field. 2–4 years of experience in higher education student services, event planning, program coordination, or student affairs. Exceptional interpersonal warmth and genuine ease with students - this is the most student-facing non-coaching role in the Division; the right candidate is energized by student interaction, not just tolerant of it. Demonstrated experience planning student-facing events that draw genuine attendance - not just coordinating logistics but designing experiences students actually choose to attend; the candidate should be able to speak to specific events they designed and what made students show up. Proficiency in reading and understanding data at both the little-picture and the big-picture level - seeing which individual student needs a call AND which populations the Division is systematically failing to reach; both skills required, not optional. Comfort with student success platforms (Watermark SS&E, EdSights, or equivalent) or clear demonstrated ability to learn quickly Strong organizational skills; ability to manage multiple programs and deadlines simultaneously without losing sight of individual students Commitment to the Catholic and Basilian mission of UST and the holistic development of every Celt Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in Higher Education, Student Affairs, Counseling, Education, or a related field. Four or more years of progressively responsible experience in student services, student success, academic support, advising, retention, or program coordination. Demonstrated experience planning, implementing, and assessing a first-year experience program, summer bridge program, student success institute, or other cohort-based student success initiative as the primary student-facing coordinator. Experience utilizing student contact tracking systems, early alert platforms, advising technologies, or case management tools, with a demonstrated commitment to translating student data into timely and meaningful student outreach. Bilingual proficiency in English and Spanish strongly preferred. Experience supporting first-generation, low-income, DACA-eligible, veteran, underrepresented, or otherwise underserved student populations. Experience with student success and advising platforms such as Watermark SS&E, EAB Navigate, EdSights, or similar student engagement technologies. Demonstrated commitment to student persistence, retention, belonging, and academic success in a diverse higher education environment. Work Environment and Schedule This is a student-facing position that follows the University's standard working hours requirements. Regular availability and presence during established office hours are essential to providing reliable support to students, faculty, staff and campus partners. Employees receive vacation time for rest, recuperation, and personal matters. Because of the nature and scope of this position, vacation scheduling should be coordinated to minimize conflicts with important meetings, events, scheduled office activities, and critical operational deadlines. Mission and Values The University of St. Thomas is a private Catholic liberal arts university in Houston, Texas, guided by the Basilian values of Goodness, Discipline, and Knowledge. UST serves approximately 3,200 students and holds Hispanic-Serving Institution (HSI) designation. A significant portion of our students are first-generation college students - and the Division of Student Success coordinates the institutional response to ensure every Celt has a clear pathway to graduate, flourish, and live out their calling. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/3658339": {
      "title": "Core Adjunct Faculty",
      "description": "Core Adjunct Faculty. University of St. Thomas. Core Adjunct Faculty The Division of the Core and Centers for Excellence at the University of St. Thomas seeks qualified applicants for as-needed adjunct faculty positions to teach core liberal arts classes in Philosophy, History, English, Theology, and the Classical Trivium. Our core curriculum is committed to the classical ideal of liberal education as philosophical and non-utilitarian. It embraces the approach to liberal learning embodied in the writings of St. John Henry Newman, Josef Pieper, and James Schall. More information about our core curriculum is available here. Applicants must possess at least a master's degree in Philosophy, History, English, Theology, or a related field. We encourage applications from those who are able to teach courses in person, but are also interested in applicants who are willing and able to teach online. Interested applicants should complete the application, submit a CV, cover letter, and official transcripts to the office of the Division Dean of Liberal Studies. Two letters of recommendation should be sent separately to the Associate Division Dean by recommenders. Email all materials to Erik van Versendaal (erik.vanversendaal@stthom.edu). The University of St. Thomas is an Equal Opportunity Employer"
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4231133": {
      "title": "Dean, Cameron School of Business",
      "description": "Dean, Cameron School of Business. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS DEAN, CAMERON SCHOOL OF BUSINESS CAMERON SCHOOL OF BUSINESS The University of St. Thomas (UST) announces a national search for the position of Dean of the Cameron School of Business, with employment to begin July 1, 2027. As UST works to increase enrollment and strengthen its standing as a major institution of higher education, we seek a Dean who will ensure the delivery of high-quality programs and instruction, foster mission-focused faculty development, and guide the strategic growth of the school while remaining faithful to the University's distinctive Catholic mission. UST prepares students for success in today's dynamic economy through a strong core curriculum in the liberal arts, exceptionally talented and dedicated faculty, and innovative co-curricular programs. An independent, coeducational private university, UST enrolls nearly 4,000 students (more than 3,000 undergraduates and almost 700 graduate students) from 34 states and 61 countries. We are Houston's only Catholic university, welcoming students of all faiths and backgrounds, and our student body reflects the city we serve. Houston is the nation's fourth-largest city and home to one of the most dynamic business environments in the world: a global center of energy, health care and life sciences, international trade, shipping and logistics, aerospace, and finance, with more than twenty Fortune 500 headquarters. The Cameron School of Business is located in the heart of this economy and is uniquely positioned to serve it. The Cameron School of Business offers undergraduate and graduate programs in business administration, management, marketing, accounting, finance, economics, and international business, including the Master of Business Administration (MBA) and a nationally recognized Master in Clinical Translation Management. The Dean's primary responsibility is stewardship of the school's academic performance and enrollment growth. The Dean will also serve as the school's principal ambassador to the Houston business community, cultivating partnerships with corporations, professional firms, entrepreneurs, alumni, and nonprofit organizations; ensuring that the school's programs and faculty scholarship respond to the needs of the region's employers; and maintaining the school's accreditations. The Dean reports directly to the Provost and serves as the chief academic and administrative officer of the Cameron School of Business. Stewardship and Strategic Role Functions Develop and lead a clear strategic vision for the Cameron School of Business, aligned with the University's strategic plan and Catholic mission, and integrate it into the school's academic planning and operations. Guide the strategic development, implementation, and evaluation of the school's academic programs to ensure their quality, relevance, and market competitiveness. Lead enrollment growth across the school, with particular emphasis on expanding the MBA program and other graduate offerings, by working closely with Enrollment Management and Marketing on recruitment strategy, program positioning, and yield. Ensure that the school's degree programs, concentrations, certificates, and executive education offerings are aligned with the workforce needs of the Houston and Gulf Coast business community, including energy, health care, finance, logistics, and technology, and lead the development of new programs where demand warrants. Foster the conditions and relationships that support creativity, innovation, and the formation of learning communities among faculty and students. Support faculty and staff professional development at both the individual and the departmental level. External Relations and Community Engagement Serve as the principal representative of the Cameron School of Business to the Houston business community, actively building and maintaining relationships with corporate executives, professional associations, chambers of commerce, and civic organizations. Lead a Dean's Business Advisory Council of regional executives to inform curriculum, provide internships and placement opportunities for students, and strengthen the school's reputation. Expand internship, mentorship, experiential learning, and career placement pipelines for students through employer partnerships. Work with Institutional Advancement to cultivate donors, secure gifts, endowed chairs, scholarships, and sponsored programs, and steward the school's benefactors and namesake legacy. Engage alumni as partners in recruitment, mentoring, philanthropy, and career placement. Support/Oversight Functions: Faculty and Department Chairs Recruit, develop, evaluate, and retain a high-quality faculty committed to teaching excellence, the University's mission, and the professional development of students. Develop and implement plans and allocate resources for faculty scholarship that addresses the needs of the region's business community and contributes to the school's AACSB standing. Encourage faculty engagement with industry through consulting, executive education, applied research partnerships, and service on corporate and civic boards. Create, implement, and evaluate the processes and procedures essential to an effective and collaborative academic enterprise, including faculty workload, evaluation, promotion, and tenure. Support/Oversight Functions: Academic Programs Collaborate with the other deans and with colleagues in Finance, Enrollment Management, Student Affairs, Institutional Advancement, Marketing, and Human Resources to develop and support programs that contribute to sustainable growth and high-quality learning outcomes. Oversee the selection, development, and orientation of department chairs. Facilitate the growth and enhancement of academic programs, both online and face-to-face, through support for sponsored research, grants, and partnerships. Oversee the school's assessment of student learning and the use of assessment results for continuous improvement. Ensure that curricula remain current with respect to technology, data analytics, and the evolving practices of the business professions. Academic Leadership Provide academic leadership within the school and across the University, and serve as a member of the Council of Deans. Ensure that academic program reports and proposals presented to the Provost meet appropriate quality standards to support effective review, decision-making, and implementation. Model and promote a culture of collegiality, shared governance, and accountability. Administrative and Regulatory Functions Direct the work of department chairs and staff to ensure that the school's goals and objectives are met, including enrollment, retention, graduation, and placement goals. Oversee resource acquisition and the school's budget, including responsible fiscal management and the development of new revenue streams. Ensure compliance with the accreditation standards and reporting requirements of The Commission on Colleges and Universities (CCU, formerly SACSCOC) and The Association to Advance Collegiate Schools of Business (AACSB), and lead the school's continuous improvement review process. Ensure consistent application of University policies relevant to the school's faculty and academic mission. Qualifications Education: Candidates may qualify through either of two career paths: Academic path: an earned terminal degree (Ph.D., D.B.A., or equivalent) in a field represented in the Cameron School of Business or a related field, with a record of teaching and scholarly achievement sufficient to hold rank as a tenured associate or full professor. Executive/entrepreneurial path: a master's degree in business or a related field (M.B.A. or equivalent) is required; a terminal degree is preferred but not required. Experience and Other Attributes: Candidates may qualify through either of two career paths: Academic path: substantial teaching experience and a minimum of five years of senior-level academic administrative experience (department chair, associate dean, dean, or equivalent); or Executive/entrepreneurial path: a minimum of ten years of senior leadership experience in business, strategic leadership of an enterprise or major division, or the founding and successful growth of a company; prior teaching experience is welcome but not required. Under either path, the successful candidate will demonstrate an understanding of academic culture, shared governance, and accreditation, and a commitment to teaching and student success. Demonstrated success in growing enrollment, particularly in graduate and MBA programs. Demonstrated ability to build productive relationships with corporate, professional, and civic leaders, and a record of successful fundraising or external partnership development. A deep understanding of and respect for the liberal arts as the foundation for quality professional education. Proven ability to inspire, collaborate, and negotiate with varied and sometimes competing constituencies; the successful candidate must be able to lead the faculty to new levels of engagement and to champion creativity and innovation. Experience with CCU and AACSB accreditation preferred. An experienced leader with a clear and focused commitment to teaching, learning, and academic excellence in support of student success. A seasoned professional experienced in shared governance and committed to fostering cohesion, collegiality, and accountability among faculty and staff. Demonstrated knowledge, skills, and abilities in academic administration, including applicable regulations, laws, and policies. A demonstrated record of fiscal responsibility and accountability, with a high degree of financial acumen. Strong collegial leadership qualities, including the demonstrated ability to recruit and build a strong faculty. Recognized academic vision, with attention to the future of higher education, emerging trends in business education, and the changing needs of employers. Familiarity with online and hybrid program delivery, executive education, and data-driven program management. Viewed as highly ethical, trustworthy, credible, and respectful of differing views and opinions, and known for building consensus and being a good listener. Highly effective communication skills, both verbal and written. The Dean must demonstrate a genuine understanding of and commitment to the Catholic intellectual tradition, the dialogue between faith and reason, the principles of Ex corde Ecclesiae, and the Basilian values of goodness, discipline, and knowledge. The Dean must also be willing and able to promote the University's Catholic identity in the daily life of the school. The University will actively seek, and strongly prefers, practicing Catholics for this role. Qualified candidates of other faith traditions who can credibly make and keep that commitment are encouraged to apply and will receive full consideration. Application Applicants should submit 1) a comprehensive letter of application addressing the responsibilities and qualifications described above ; 2) a current curriculum vitae ; 3) a statement demonstrating willingness to support the tenets of Catholic higher education as articulated in Ex c orde Ecclesiae ; 4) the names and contact information of five ; and (5) professional references. References will not be contacted without the candidate's prior consent. Review of Applications begin s December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4246092": {
      "title": "Dean, School of Education and Human Services",
      "description": "Dean, School of Education and Human Services. University of St. Thomas. THE UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS DEAN, SCHOOL OF EDUCATION AND HUMAN SERVICES The University of St. Thomas (UST) announces a national search for the position of Dean of the School of Education and Human Services (SEHS), with employment to begin July 1, 2027. As UST works to increase enrollment and strengthen its standing in the field of higher education, we seek a Dean who will develop and administer high-quality programs and instruction, foster mission-focused faculty development, and guide the strategic growth of the school in a manner that supports the University's distinctive Catholic mission. UST prepares students for success in today's dynamic landscape through a strong core curriculum, exceptionally talented and dedicated faculty, and innovative co-curricular programs. An independent, coeducational private university, UST enrolls nearly 4,000 students (more than 3,000 undergraduates and almost 700 graduate students). We are Houston's only Catholic university and welcome students of all faiths and backgrounds. Located in the fourth largest city in America, UST spans almost 20 city blocks in the heart of Houston's highly desirable Museum District/Innovation Corridor. The city is known for its fine and performing arts culture, leading medical and research facilities, a large port, winning sports teams, and abundant professional opportunities. The campus is minutes from the renowned Texas Medical Center, the largest in the world, of which UST is a member institution. The vibrant downtown, with its many restaurants, theaters, concert halls and sports arenas, is also in close proximity. Academic Programs UST has five schools: the School of Arts and Sciences; the Peavy School of Nursing; the Cameron School of Business; the School of Theology; and the School of Education and Human Services as well as a number of Centers for Excellence including the Nesti Center for Faith and Culture, the Center for International Studies, the Center for Irish Studies, the St. John Paul II Institute, and the Center for Thomistic Studies. The University is served by close to 350 dedicated full-time and part-time faculty with 93% possessing a terminal degree. The School of Education and Human Services encompasses three distinct yet complementary academic disciplines: Education Clinical Mental Health Counseling Criminology, Law and Society Its academic offerings include: Two undergraduate pre-professional programs in Criminology, Law, and Society and General Studies: Education An AAS in Pragmatic Studies and BAAS in Integrated Studies designed specifically for students who have learning differences that may interfere with academic success in a traditional collegiate setting Four graduate degree programs: Master of Science in Clinical Mental Health Counseling Master of Education Doctor of Education MA in Criminology, Law and Society The School of Education and Human Services (SEHS) is committed to preparing ethical professionals who serve their communities through education, counseling, leadership, and public service. Rooted in the University's Catholic intellectual tradition and Basilian charisms of goodness, discipline, and knowledge, the School of Education and Human Services is committed not only to respecting the distinctive contributions of each academic discipline but also seeking a higher synthesis of knowledge grounded in the mutually enriching dialog between faith and reason. The Dean of the School of Education provides leadership in the following key functions: Stewardship and Strategic Role Functions Develop and lead a clear strategic vision for the School of Education and Human Services, aligned with the University's strategic plan and Catholic mission, and integrate it into the school's academic planning and operations. Guide the strategic development, implementation, and evaluation of the school's academic programs to ensure their quality, relevance, and market competitiveness. Develop a strategy for the school to collaborate with and respond to the needs of regional Catholic schools and the growing classical and liberal arts K-12 movement in the United States and beyond. Create and maintain relationships with key mission aligned partners such as Texas Conference of Catholic Bishops' Education Department. Lead enrollment growth across the school, with an emphasis on expanding its graduate offerings. Ensure that the school's degree programs, concentrations, certificates, and executive education offerings are aligned with the workforce needs of the Houston professional community. Foster the conditions and relationships that support creativity, innovation, and the formation of learning communities among faculty and students. Expand donor funded scholarship opportunities and celebrate the achievements of our students in ways that inspire the community. Support/Oversight Functions: Faculty and Department Chairs Foster a collaborative and supportive academic environment that promotes faculty excellence in teaching, scholarship, service, and professional development. Provide leadership, mentorship, and administrative support to department chairs and program directors, ensuring they have the resources and guidance necessary to effectively lead their respective academic disciplines. Support faculty recruitment, retention, evaluation, and professional advancement, including opportunities for scholarly engagement, research, continuing education, and leadership development. Promote shared governance, transparent communication, and meaningful faculty participation in academic decision-making, while respecting the distinct needs and contributions of the School's academic disciplines. Ensure equitable and effective allocation of faculty resources, including teaching assignments, workload distribution, and support for scholarly and service activities. Foster a culture of collegiality, interdisciplinary collaboration, and continuous improvement that reflects the University's Catholic mission and commitment to academic excellence. Support/Oversight Functions: Academic Programs Provide strategic oversight of the School's undergraduate and graduate programs, ensuring academic quality, curricular relevance, and alignment with the University's mission and strategic priorities. Collaborate with department chairs, program directors, faculty, and University leadership to evaluate, strengthen, and expand academic offerings, including traditional, online, and hybrid programs, in response to student needs, professional standards, and emerging workforce demands. Promote student recruitment, retention, progression, and successful degree completion through collaboration with Enrollment Management, Student Affairs, and other University departments. Strengthen and expand community partnerships, field experiences, clinical placements, internships, and other experiential learning opportunities that prepare students for professional practice and service. Encourage interdisciplinary collaboration among Education, Clinical Mental Health Counseling, and Criminology, Law, and Society, creating opportunities for innovative programming, scholarship, and community engagement. Facilitate sustainable program growth through the development of new academic offerings, external partnerships, sponsored research, grants, and other initiatives that enhance the School's visibility and impact. Administrative and Regulatory Functions Oversee resource allocation, resource acquisition, and responsible management of the School's budget in alignment with academic and strategic priorities. Ensure compliance with University, state, federal, and institutional regulatory requirements and reporting obligations, including requirements associated with The Commission on Colleges and Universities (CCU, formerly SACSCOC). Provide leadership and administrative support for the maintenance of CACREP accreditation for the Clinical Mental Health Counseling program, including ongoing compliance, assessment, reporting, and accreditation review processes. Provide leadership for the pursuit of Texas Education Agency (TEA) accreditation/approval and, once obtained, ensure continued compliance with applicable TEA standards, reporting requirements, and educator preparation regulations. The Dean will serve as the designated EPP Legal Authority Address and ensure required curriculum documentation demonstrating alignment of coursework and training with discipline specific standards. Support department chairs and program leadership in maintaining compliance with other applicable professional, licensure, certification, and regulatory standards across the School's academic programs. Ensure timely and accurate completion of required institutional, accreditation, and regulatory reports and facilitate appropriate documentation and continuous improvement processes. Ensure consistent and equitable application of University policies and procedures relevant to faculty, staff, students, academic programs, and the School's academic mission. Qualifications: Education and Experience Terminal degree in a field represented in Education, Counseling, Criminology/Criminal Justice, or related fields with a solid reputation for excellence and scholarly achievement. Seven years of full-time, higher education teaching experience and at least five years of previous administrative experience (department chair, associate dean, dean, or equivalent) preferred. The ideal candidate exemplifies both interpersonal excellence and professional effectiveness, bringing a leadership style that is collaborative, respectful, transparent, and grounded in strong communication. The candidate should demonstrate a commitment to integrating all disciplines in the school into the broader tradition of Catholic liberal education. The candidate should be an attentive listener who values diverse perspectives, builds trust and consensus, navigates conflict and competing priorities effectively, and inspires faculty and staff toward greater engagement, creativity, and innovation. Equally important, the candidate should demonstrate strong organizational and administrative leadership, with the ability to manage responsibilities reliably, communicate clearly and consistently, and ensure appropriate follow-through and accountability. The ideal candidate will bring knowledge of academic governance, accreditation, regulations and policies, fiscal management, faculty recruitment and development, and other areas essential to the effective operation of the School. They should demonstrate a clear commitment to teaching, learning, academic excellence, and student success, while bringing a forward-looking vision that prepares the School to respond thoughtfully to emerging trends and opportunities in higher education. The Dean must demonstrate a genuine understanding of and commitment to the Catholic intellectual tradition, the dialogue between faith and reason, the principles of Ex Corde Ecclesiae, and the Basilian charisms of goodness, discipline, and knowledge. The Dean must also be willing and able to promote the University's Catholic identity in the daily life of the school. The University will actively seek, and strongly prefers, practicing Catholics for this role. Qualified candidates of other faith traditions who can credibly make and keep that commitment are encouraged to apply and will receive full consideration. Applicants should submit: A comprehensive letter of application addressing the responsibilities and qualifications described above. A current curriculum vitae. A 1-to-2-page letter explaining how Ex Corde Ecclesiae will guide your leadership of the School of Education and Human Services. The names and contact information of five professional references. References will not be contacted without the candidate's prior consent. Review of applications begins December 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4246138": {
      "title": "Director, Grants Administration",
      "description": "Director, Grants Administration. University of St. Thomas. THE UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS DIRECTOR OF GRANTS ADMINISTRATION The University of St. Thomas is the Catholic University in the heart of Houston. We are committed to the Catholic intellectual tradition and the dialogue between faith and reason. By pursuing excellence in teaching, scholarship, and service, we embody and instill in our students the core values of our founders, the Basilian Fathers: goodness, discipline, and knowledge. We foster engagement in a diverse, collaborative community. The University of St. Thomas is a comprehensive university, grounded in the liberal arts. Committed to the unity of all knowledge, we offer programs in the traditional liberal arts, professional, and skill-based disciplines. University of St. Thomas graduates think critically, communicate effectively, succeed professionally, and lead ethically. JOB SUMMARY: The Director of Grants Administration leads the university's sponsored research and restricted grants portfolio, balancing rigorous institutional compliance with proactive faculty enablement. Operating under a dual framework, this position reports to the Provost's Office and works closely with the Chief Financial Officer's (CFO) office to ensure seamless alignment on fiscal matters, budget forecasting, drawdowns, and audit compliance. The Director will provide comprehensive oversight of the entire grant lifecycle, supporting faculty with pre-award proposal development and budget construction while ensuring post-award financial accuracy, audit readiness, and timely closeout. The Director serves as a strategic partner to academic departments, actively facilitating external funding while safeguarding the university's regulatory and financial standing. Essential Duties and Responsibilities: Actively monitor federal, state, and private foundation grant opportunities to systematically match them with faculty research profiles and STEM initiatives. Provide hands-on, concierge-level assistance to faculty and Principal Investigators (PIs) through the pre-award phase by translating complex RFP guidelines. Build compliant proposal budgets and manage the internal institutional routing process for all external funding requests. Design and execute ongoing training sessions, toolkits, and workshops to demystify the grant lifecycle for early-career faculty. Streamline internal workflows to actively reduce bureaucratic friction and administrative burdens for teaching and research professors. Ensure absolute institutional compliance with Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200), federal cost principles, state regulations, and individual donor restrictions. Partner closely with the general accounting, finance, and CFO offices to monitor restricted fund expenditures, ensure matching fund tracking, and review budget-to-actual reports. Execute accurate, timely federal drawdowns and oversee post-award effort certification workflows. Serve as the primary point of contact for all internal and external grant audits to guarantee zero major findings. Author, update, and implement standard operating procedures (SOPs) governing institutional research compliance, time-and-effort reporting, and conflict of interest disclosures. Qualifications: Extensive professional experience in grants administration, sponsored research, or public-sector compliance within a higher education setting. Comprehensive knowledge of Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200) and federal/state grant regulations. Proven track record of successful pre-award budgeting and post-award financial reconciliation in partnership with centralized finance teams. Demonstrated commitment to a philosophy of service, partnership, and collaboration with academic faculty. Exceptional interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills to effectively bridge administrative compliance and academic innovation. Experience working in a fast-paced, multi-faceted environment with the ability to manage strict, competing grant deadlines under pressure. Proficiency working with computer-based higher education ERP systems, grant routing software, and standard office suites. Certified Grants Management Specialist (CGMS) or Certified Research Administrator (CRA) designation (preferred). EDUCATION: Bachelor's degree required; Master's degree or advanced professional certification in a related field preferred. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Occasional evening or weekend work is necessary to meet strict external grant submission deadlines. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4230026": {
      "title": "Director, Military Relations",
      "description": "Director, Military Relations. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS DIRECTOR, MILITARY RELATIONS VETERAN SUCCESS CENTER We are the University of St. Thomas, the Catholic University in the heart of Houston. We are committed to the Catholic intellectual tradition and the dialogue between faith and reason. By pursuing excellence in teaching, scholarship and service, we embody and instill in our students the core values of our founders, the Basilian Fathers: goodness, discipline and knowledge. We foster engagement in a diverse, collaborative community. The University of St. Thomas is a comprehensive university, grounded in the liberal arts. Committed to the unity of all knowledge, we offer programs in the traditional liberal arts, professional, and skill-based disciplines. Graduates of the University of St. Thomas think critically, communicate effectively, and succeed professionally and lead ethically . A resume and cover letter must be submitted to be considered for this position. JOB SUMMARY The Director of Military Relations provides strategic leadership for the University of St. Thomas' military and veteran recruitment, outreach, and community engagement efforts. The Director is responsible for expanding UST's enrollment of veterans and military-connected students through intentional recruitment strategies at the local, regional, and national levels while strengthening the University's presence and reputation within the broader veteran community. Serving as a highly visible external representative of the University, the Director develops and sustains meaningful relationships with military installations, transition assistance programs, veteran service organizations, government agencies, community partners, employers, nonprofit organizations, and other entities serving veterans and military-connected individuals. The Director will develop a robust recruitment pipeline that introduces prospective veteran students to UST well before enrollment and creates intentional opportunities for prospective and incoming students to experience the University community. This includes developing signature recruitment and engagement events, veteran-specific campus visit experiences, incoming student programs, community stakeholder events, and other initiatives designed to position UST as a university of choice for veterans and military-connected students nationwide. The Director works collaboratively with Enrollment Management, Admissions, the Veteran Success Center, Marketing and Communications, academic programs, and other University partners to create a coordinated approach to veteran recruitment, enrollment, transition, and engagement. This position requires an individual who is highly relational, externally focused, comfortable developing partnerships and representing the University publicly, and capable of translating relationships into measurable enrollment and community-engagement outcomes. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Military and Veteran Recruitment Develop and execute a comprehensive national recruitment strategy to increase enrollment of veterans, transitioning service members, active-duty personnel, reservists, National Guard members, and other military-connected students. Build recruitment pipelines through military installations, Transition Assistance Programs (TAP), education centers, veteran-serving organizations, community colleges, military education networks, and other veteran-serving entities. Represent UST at military and veteran recruitment events, education fairs, transition events, conferences, community gatherings, and other recruitment opportunities throughout Texas and nationwide. Develop targeted recruitment strategies for geographic markets and military populations with strong potential for UST enrollment. Create consistent outreach and follow-up strategies that move prospective veteran students from initial contact through application, admission, and enrollment. Collaborate with Admissions and Enrollment Management to monitor veteran recruitment pipelines, applications, conversion, yield, and enrollment outcomes. Partner with Marketing and Communications to develop veteran-specific campaigns, digital outreach, promotional materials, storytelling, and messaging that communicate the value of a UST education. Veteran Community Relations and Strategic Partnerships Serve as a primary external ambassador for UST within the Houston-area veteran and military community. Develop and sustain relationships with veteran service organizations, military organizations, government agencies, nonprofits, healthcare and behavioral health organizations, employers, community leaders, veteran advocates, and other veteran-serving organizations. Maintain a strong UST presence throughout the Greater Houston veteran community through community events, advisory groups, professional associations, veteran initiatives, and collaborative programs. Identify opportunities for strategic partnerships, referral relationships, articulation agreements, community collaborations, and other initiatives that strengthen veteran enrollment and engagement. Develop relationships with organizational leaders and key stakeholders who can expand UST's reach within the veteran community. Establish a systematic approach to partner engagement through regular communication, campus opportunities, follow-up, and assessment of outcomes. Signature Events and Community Engagement Develop and execute large-scale signature events that bring veteran leaders, military organizations, community partners, prospective students, alumni, employers, and other key stakeholders to UST. Create an annual calendar of veteran and military engagement events that increases UST's visibility and establishes the University as a gathering place for Houston's veteran community. Host stakeholder events, networking opportunities, veteran leadership gatherings, educational programs, community conversations, and partnership events that deepen relationships between UST and the veteran community. Develop recruitment-focused events for prospective veterans and transitioning service members, including veteran open houses, information sessions, campus experiences, and academic program showcases. Coordinate with University leadership, academic programs, Advancement, Alumni Relations, Marketing, and other campus partners to ensure institutional participation in signature veteran events. Incoming Veteran Engagement and Enrollment Yield Develop intentional programming for newly admitted and incoming veteran students that introduces them to UST before their first semester and strengthens their connection to the University. Create veteran-specific admitted student experiences, welcome events, campus visits, information sessions, and relationship-building opportunities designed to increase enrollment yield. Connect incoming veterans with current veteran students, faculty, staff, alumni, academic leaders, and the Veteran Success Center prior to enrollment. Partner with the Veteran Success Center and other campus offices to ensure a seamless transition from recruitment and admission into UST's veteran support infrastructure. Maintain intentional communication with admitted veteran students between admission and matriculation to strengthen engagement and reduce enrollment melt. Strategy, Assessment, and Institutional Collaboration Establish annual goals and measurable performance indicators for veteran recruitment, enrollment, partnerships, events, community engagement, and prospective student conversion. Maintain accurate records of recruitment activity, prospective student pipelines, community partnerships, events, referrals, and enrollment outcomes. Use enrollment and engagement data to evaluate recruitment strategies, identify emerging markets, and improve results. Provide regular reports to University leadership on recruitment activity, enrollment trends, partnerships, community engagement, and progress toward established goals. Collaborate closely with the Veteran Success Center to ensure recruitment messaging accurately reflects UST's veteran services, resources, benefits support, and student experience. Work across University divisions to integrate military and veteran recruitment into broader institutional enrollment and community-engagement strategies. Perform other duties and strategic initiatives related to military and veteran recruitment and community engagement as assigned. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Minimum of four (4) years of experience in higher education admissions/recruitment, military or veteran services, or a combination thereof; or four (4) years of active-duty military service combined with relevant higher education experience. Strong communication (verbal and written)skills, vibrant personality, and strong problem-solving skills are essential. Ability to work in a goal-driven and measured performance environment. Possessing high energy, attention to detail, and the ability to organize effectively. Can manage multitasking and priorities as it pertains to engagements and recruitment. A valid driver's license and must be able to work extended hours or weekends during peak times and for special programs, projects, and events. Be able to work on site at the Veterans Success Center within the standard hours of operation (Monday through Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 5 p.m.) if not otherwise tasked with an external work responsibility. Able to travel and work flexible hours, including nights and weekends. EDUCATION Bachelor's degree required. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master's degree. Prior military service Experience using customer relationship management (CRM) platforms to support recruitment, outreach, and relationship management. Experience planning and executing large-scale events, recruitment programs, or community engagement initiatives. PHYSICAL DEMANDS This is a student-facing position that follows the University's standard working hours requirements. Regular availability and presence during established office hours are essential to providing reliable support to students, faculty, and campus partners. Evening and weekend work may be necessary. Some out-of-state travel may be required. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4196880": {
      "title": "Director, Residence Life",
      "description": "Director, Residence Life. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS DIRECTOR OF RESIDENCE LIFE RESIDENCE LIFE Introduction to the University of St. Thomas The University of St. Thomas (UST) is a private Catholic University committed to the liberal arts and the religious and intellectual tradition of Catholic higher education, located in Houston, Texas, near the vibrant central business district, Texas Medical Center, and museum district. The University considers candidates in light of their professional credentials, experience, and compatibility with the University's Catholic identity and purpose. UST is committed to hiring individuals who can help to advance its integrated mission-the formation of the whole person for flourishing in this life and the next-and give public witness to that mission in new ways. The University's Catholic identity is at the center of this mission, a Catholicism expressed for our own age by the Catechism of the Catholic Church (1992), and Pope Saint John Paul II's Ex Corde Ecclesiae. The University asks all candidates applying for a position to consider these documents to ensure that their professional goals, practices, and values can align with them. Applicant for this position must be Catholic, and they need to be able to support joyfully, professionally, and publicly the University as it seeks to be faithful to and embody both a robust Catholicism--in its intellectual, doctrinal, moral, and spiritual dimensions. Position Summary The Director of Residence Life is responsible for the daily operations, supervision, and management of Residence Life. The Director reports to the Assistant Dean of Campus Life and is appointed with the approval of the Vice-President for Student Affairs and the President of the University. Essential Responsibilities Housing Operations Manage the leasing process for all residence spaces (residence halls, apartments, and leased properties) including: housing assignments, occupancy, marketing, prospective resident communication, room and roommate assignments, consolidation, etc. Maintain accurate housing data and ensure occupancy goals are met Oversee the Residence Life operations calendar and develop, track, and report measures of progress toward departmental and university strategic goals Partner with Facilities to oversee the maintenance, appearance, safety, cleanliness, and functionality of residential facilities Coordinate housing processes with campus partners as needed Budget Management Manage and track the housing budget (approx. $3M), including expense coding and budget allocation Monitor expenses, correct errors, and ensure proper use of funds. Policies and Conduct Develop, update, and enforce housing policies Oversee the student conduct process related to housing Ensure policies are reviewed annually and updated as needed Staff Supervision and Development Hire, train, and directly supervise three to five professional staff members Lead hiring, training, and indirect supervision of 30-40 student staff members Provide regular feedback and address performance or conduct issues in consultation with the Assistant Dean of Campus Life Lead staff development and training programs for Student Support and Community Building Create a residential environment that supports student growth, leadership development, and active engagement Build relationships with students and stay connected to the student experience Serve on campus committees as needed (e.g., Care Team, residence hall planning groups) Partner with Counseling and other offices to support students in crisis Work with Accessibility services to provide appropriate housing accommodations On-Call and Emergency Response Serve as an on-call resource for Residence Life professional and student staff Respond to emergencies and serious incidents (e.g., mental health crises, Title IX, substance use) and, when appropriate, direct the work of professional and student staff during the response Coordinate with campus partners such as UST Police and Counseling as needed General Responsibilities Support, interpret and enforce University policies, procedures and regulations Participate in professional development activities Complete additional duties assigned by the Assistant Dean of Campus Life Qualifications and Requirements Required Must be a practicing Catholic in good standing Strong communication and interpersonal skills Excellent interpersonal skills for work with students, faculty, and staff across campus Proficiency in Microsoft Office Willingness to live in University-provided housing At least 3 years of professional experience in housing or student affairs Preferred Master's degree in Higher Education, Student Affairs, or related field Experience with StarRez and/or Maxient Additional Information $56,000–$60,000 annual salary. The role requires living on campus. The Director receives a 2-bedroom townhome with rent, utilities, Wi-Fi, and maintenance included in the compensation package. Additional compensation includes cell phone bill reimbursement, a meal plan, and tuition assistance. This is a full-time, 12-month position with full benefits. The position is overtime exempt. Evening and weekend work is occasionally required. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4054581": {
      "title": "Full-Time Non-Tenure Track Faculty, Clinical Mental Health Counseling",
      "description": "Full-Time Non-Tenure Track Faculty, Clinical Mental Health Counseling. University of St. Thomas. University of St. Thomas-Houston School of Education and Human Services Faculty Position in Clinical Mental Health Counseling Full-Time Non-Tenure Track Faculty Position The Clinical Mental Health Counseling (CMCH) Graduate program at the University of St. Thomas in Houston, Texas, announces one full-time, non-tenure track (10-month) faculty position. Start date for the position is August 1, 2026. We are seeking an individual who shares our vision for training highly competent and effective professional counselors who uphold the highest standards of the profession and encourage the dialogue between faith and reason. The CMHC program prepares students to serve the community as licensed professional counselors (LPCs). Graduates of the Master of Science (M.S.) in CMHC meet the educational and practical requirements the Texas State Board of Examiners of Professional Counselors (TSBEPC) for eligibility for LPC licensure. The M.S. in Clinical Mental Health Counseling is accredited by CACREP. The M.S. in CMHC program upholds the core values of the university and professional counseling, with a particular emphasis on the promotion of the highest standards of ethical, legal, and professional conduct; evidence-based clinical practice; respect for and ability to engage with socially and culturally diverse backgrounds; respect for the dignity of all persons; and the ongoing dialogue between faith and reason. The program is poised for leadership and service in Houston, one of America's most culturally diverse centers. Further, the department fosters the growth of students, faculty, and staff, and faculty members receive supportive mentoring from senior faculty. The position responsibilities include: Teaching 7 graduate-level courses per year (usually 3-4 per semester) Participation, leadership, and membership within the profession and professional organizations Serving on departmental, school, and university committees Academic advising and mentoring of graduate students Developing and carrying out a productive research agenda in clinical, supervision, or education issues in the professional counseling field Applicants must: Core faculty must meet one of the following qualifications: have an earned doctoral degree in counselor education, preferably from a CACREP accredited program; or have a related doctoral degree and have been employed as a full-time faculty member in a counselor education program for a minimum of one full academic year before July 1, 2013; or have been employed as a full-time doctoral-level faculty member in a CORE-accredited master's program prior to July 1, 2017; or have graduated from a rehabilitation doctoral program prior to January 1, 2018. Be licensed or license-eligible as a licensed professional counselor (LPC) in Texas Possess advanced clinical, supervision, and teaching skills, Have knowledge of and sustained professional engagement in line with CACREP professional standards, Demonstrate the potential for an active and productive program of research, Demonstrate emerging professional leadership skills in professional counseling and/or counselor education, Demonstrate respect for and ability to engage with a diverse array of worldviews and faith traditions, Demonstrate commitment to the mission of the University as expressed in UST's Mission Statement and Ex Corde Ecclesiae. In order to be considered for this position, each of the following items must be submitted: a cover letter addressing qualifications for the position; current curriculum vitae; philosophy of teaching statement; official transcripts, three professional references, a written statement reflecting on the mission of Catholic higher education in light of Ex Corde Ecclesiae and the integration of counselor education with that mission. The University of St. Thomas is an equal opportunity employer."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4198973": {
      "title": "General Laborer",
      "description": "General Laborer. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS GENERAL LABORER FACILITIES OPERATIONS Position Summary The University of St. Thomas is seeking a reliable and hardworking General Laborer to support daily facilities operations across campus. This role is responsible for setting up and breaking down spaces for university events, moving furniture and equipment, and providing general assistance to skilled trades staff (electrical, plumbing, carpentry, HVAC, etc.). The ideal candidate is a team player who can perform physically demanding work safely and efficiently while representing the university in a professional manner. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Set up and tear down tables, chairs, staging, and equipment for university events, meetings, conferences, and ceremonies Move furniture, equipment, and materials within and between campus buildings Provide general labor support to tradespeople during maintenance, repair, and construction projects Load and unload delivery trucks and transport materials using hand trucks, dollies, or other equipment Assist with minor building maintenance tasks as directed Maintain cleanliness and organization of work and storage areas Operate hand tools, power tools, and light equipment safely Follow all safety protocols, including proper lifting techniques and use of personal protective equipment (PPE) Respond to event and operational needs during evenings, weekends, and occasional holidays as scheduled Perform other related duties as assigned by supervisor Required Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent (GED) Ability to lift and carry up to 50 lbs regularly, and up to 75+ lbs with assistance Ability to stand, walk, bend, kneel, and climb for extended periods Willingness to work a flexible schedule, including evenings and weekends as needed Strong work ethic, reliability, and ability to follow instructions Ability to work both independently and as part of a team Preferred Qualifications Prior experience in general labor, facilities, event setup, or a similar role (preferably in higher education) Basic familiarity with hand and power tools Prior experience assisting skilled trades (electrical, plumbing, carpentry, etc.) Valid driver's license Work Environment This position involves both indoor and outdoor work in varying weather conditions, and requires physical exertion including lifting, pushing, and pulling heavy items. Work may occur on ladders, scaffolding, or in confined spaces as needed. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4234912": {
      "title": "Human Resources Generalist",
      "description": "Human Resources Generalist. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS HUMAN RESOURCES GENERALIST HUMAN RESOURCES The University of St. Thomas, a Catholic liberal arts institution in Houston, Texas (UST), invites applications for the position of Human Resources Generalist. SUMMARY The Human Resources Generalist supports the daily operations of the Human Resources Office and assists in administering core HR functions across the employee lifecycle from recruitment through onboarding and ongoing employment. This position is a point of contact for faculty, staff, students, and visitors, ensuring efficient HR processes, responsive service, and a welcoming office environment. The role supports compliance with federal and state regulations while advancing the University's mission and values. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES TALENT ACQUISITION AND ONBOARDING The HR Generalist supports student recruitment activities by posting job openings and assisting hiring managers throughout the hiring process. This role facilitates pre-employment processes such as background checks and onboarding, ensuring a smooth and welcoming transition for new student employees. HR OPERATIONS AND ADMINISTRATIVE SUPPORT The HR Generalist provides general administrative support to Human Resources. This includes greeting visitors, faculty, staff, students, and applicants, in a professional and welcoming manner. This role assists in answering and screening incoming calls, responding to general inquiries, and managing the HR department's email inbox, ensuring timely and accurate communication or routing of requests to appropriate staff. This role contributes to the efficient operation of HR services and supports departmental initiatives including managing correspondence, preparing reports, and assisting with special projects. EMPLOYEE RECORDS AND HRIS ADMINISTRATION The HR Generalist maintains accurate and up-to-date employee records, both electronic and physical, ensuring confidentiality and compliance with applicable regulations. This role assists with data entry and maintenance in the HRIS, supports reporting needs, and ensures proper documentation of employment actions. EMPLOYEE RELATIONS AND SUPPORT The HR Generalist serves as a resource for employee and student worker inquiries related to HR policies, procedures, and programs. This position provides administrative support in employee relations matters, including performance management processes and documentation, escalating complex issues to HR leadership as appropriate. TRAINING AND PROFESSIONAL DEVELOPMENT SUPPORT The HR Generalist assists in coordinating training sessions and professional development programs for faculty, staff, and student employees. Responsibilities include scheduling sessions, tracking attendance, and supporting the delivery of learning initiatives aligned with institutional goals. PROFESSIONAL AND QUALITY CUSTOMER SERVICE The HR Generalist demonstrates professionalism and provides quality customer service in accordance with UST's values; maintains positive working relationships; ensures confidentiality; communicates effectively; maintains accurate records; and supports a safe and organized work environment. The position requires flexibility, attention to detail, and a willingness to learn and adapt to changing needs. QUALIFICATIONS, EXPERIENCE, SKILLS A bachelor's degree in human resources, business administration, or a related field preferred Minimum of three (3) years of professional experience in human resources or a related field required Experience in higher education preferred Familiarity with HRIS systems; ADP Workforce Now experience preferred Basic knowledge of employment laws and HR practices Proficiency in Microsoft Office products Strong organizational skills with attention to detail and accuracy Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to interact effectively with faculty, staff, students, and visitors Ability to handle sensitive and confidential information with discretion Due to the need for visibility on campus and engagement with the university community, this position is expected to work on-site during UST's regular business hours: 8:30 AM to 5:30 PM, Monday – Friday The University of St. Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/4147085": {
      "title": "Senior Accountant",
      "description": "Senior Accountant. University of St. Thomas. University of St. Thomas Senior Accountant Department: Business Office Summary The Senior Accountant is responsible for performing advanced accounting functions that support the University's financial operations. This position assists the Accounting Manager with the preparation of accurate and timely financial reports, month-end and year-end close activities, account reconciliations, audit support, and compliance with Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP) and University policies. The Senior Accountant works collaboratively with departments across the University to ensure the accuracy and integrity of financial information and provides analytical support for accounting operations, grants, endowments, and special projects. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Prepare and maintain accurate general ledger accounts and supporting schedules. Assist the Accounting Manager in preparing accurate and timely monthly, quarterly, and other periodic financial reports. Perform month-end and year-end closing activities, including journal entries, account reconciliations, and account analysis. Reconcile bank accounts, balance sheet accounts, and other financial records to ensure accuracy and completeness. Assist with the preparation of audit support PBC schedules, supporting documentation, and other auditor requests as assigned by Controller/Accounting Manager. Maintain and recommend internal controls and improvements to strengthen financial processes and ensure compliance. Assist with accounting for grants and restricted funds, including monitoring expenditures, preparing reconciliations, and supporting required financial reporting. Research and apply Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP) and University policies to accounting transactions. Research financial data and prepare ad hoc reports for management as requested. Assist in maintaining the integrity of financial data within the University's ERP system. Participate in testing and implementation of system upgrades and enhancements affecting accounting processes. Identify opportunities to improve accounting procedures and operational efficiencies. Prepare documentation for updating accounting procedures. Provide guidance and support to junior accountant and other business team members on accounting best practices as needed. Prepare reports and schedules for internal and external stakeholders as requested. Assist with special accounting projects and other duties as assigned. Qualifications and Requirements Minimum of five (5) years of progressively responsible accounting experience. Higher education or nonprofit accounting experience preferred. Strong knowledge of Generally Accepted Accounting Principles (GAAP). Experience preparing financial statements, reconciliations, and supporting audit activities. Experience with grant and fund accounting is preferred. Ability to analyze financial information, identify discrepancies, and recommend appropriate corrective action. Strong organizational skills with the ability to prioritize multiple assignments and meet deadlines. Excellent analytical, problem-solving, and attention-to-detail skills. Ability to work independently while collaborating effectively with colleagues across the University. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Ability to communicate financial information clearly to both financial and non-financial personnel. Advanced proficiency in Microsoft Excel. Experience with enterprise resource planning (ERP) financial systems; PeopleSoft experience preferred. Ability to read, analyze, and interpret professional journals, governmental regulations, and accounting guidance. Demonstrated commitment to integrity, customer service, and the mission of the University. Education Bachelor's degree (B.A. or B.S.) from an accredited four-year college or university in Accounting, Finance, Economics, or a related business field. Certification CPA highly preferred but not required. The University of Saint Thomas is committed to the religious, ethical, and intellectual traditions of Catholic higher education. As permitted by law, practicing Catholics who will advance the mission of the school are preferred for this position. However, the University invites all qualified applicants to apply ."
    },
    "https://stthom.applicantpro.com/jobs/2720163": {
      "title": "UST Police Officer - Police Department",
      "description": "UST Police Officer - Police Department. University of St. Thomas. UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS - HOUSTON DEPARTMENT: POLICE UST POLICE OFFICER The University of St. Thomas ( www.stthom.edu ), a private Catholic university committed to the liberal arts and to the religious and intellectual tradition of Catholic higher education, is seeking a Police Officer. The University enrolls more than 3,700 students with its main campus located in Houston's Museum District. The purpose of this position is to provide law enforcement service to the University of St. Thomas campus and the surrounding neighborhood. Essential Duties and Responsibilities This job title supports the operations of the University of St. Thomas and its Police Department by fulfilling the following functions: Patrol campus in radio-equipped vehicle. Protect life and property against criminal attack and preserve the peace. Provide protection against harmful acts, both willful and inadvertent of non-criminal as well as criminal class. Responsible for the security and policing of buildings. Educate and/or inform students, faculty and staff concerning parking regulations and enforce parking regulations. Maintain effective relations with faculty, staff, and students. Regulate vehicles and pedestrians in traffic in order to prevent accidents and congestion. Provide service to all persons on campus, give information, assist and comfort those in distress. Cooperate with all local law enforcement agencies, investigate all criminal acts and process the recovery of stolen property. Arrest offenders in violation of city, state, and federal laws. Enforce all applicable University regulations. Write reports on all criminal acts and violations of University regulations and make follow-up reports when required. Prepare cases for presentation in court and testify as a witness when called. Complete reports as necessary. Provides courtesy escorts on an as needed basis. Opens/Closes facilities as required. Perform other duties as required. Qualifications and Requirements Education/License Required: Graduation from an accredited high school or completion of a GED. Associates degree or higher preferred. Must be a licensed Texas Peace Officer or the ability to be licensed within 3 month s of hire. Experience: previous experience in a university police department of similar size is preferred. Working Conditions: Individuals in this position work inside and outside in all types of weather; are exposed to dirt, dust, noise, fumes, odors, heat, cold, rain, humidity, and sudden temperature changes; frequently are exposed to mechanical hazards, traffic hazards, and bodily injuries; are infrequently exposed to chemical, electronic, and fire hazards, explosives, and contagious diseases. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Knowledge of functions and obligations of law enforcement. Ability to direct traffic and regulate traffic and parking problems. Knowledge of the use and care of firearms. Ability to exercise clear, quick thinking in emergencies. Demonstrate initiative and good judgment. Ability to deal with people with a firm and tactful manner. Ability to meet the public courteously and intelligently. Ability to write concise, intelligible reports. Ability to understand and follow oral and written instructions. Knowledge of how to apply first aid. Ability to cooperate with representatives of other law enforcement agencies. Ability to maintain effective relations with faculty, staff, and students. Must be willing to work at night, on weekends, and to report for duty any time an emergency arises. Must be available to work various shifts with rotating days off. Essential functions or requirements listed above may not be performed in every position with this title, and these essential functions may not include all related duties that might be requested and/or performed. General Requirements: Must not be less than 21 years of age. Satisfactory results from written test, interview process, background investigation, agility test required. Must have or obtain a valid Texas motor vehicle operator's license. Heavy lifting, exerting 50-100 lbs. occasionally, 10-25 lbs. frequently, or up to 10-20 lbs. constantly. Constantly sits, sees, speaks, and drives. Frequently stands, walks, kneels, crouches, squats, twists body, lifts heavy objects and people; pushes vehicles; and writes by hand. Infrequently crawls, climbs, runs, fights, and wrestles. Vision must be correctable to 20/20. Must have normal hearing. The above statements are intended to describe the general nature of work performed by individuals in this job classification. They are not intended to be construed as an exhaustive list of all responsibilities, duties, and qualifications required of individuals in this job classification. All job classifications may have miscellaneous job duties assigned on an as needed basis. Management, at the appropriate level, retains the right to assign and change the duties of any position at any time. The University of St. Thomas is an Equal Opportunity Employer"
    }
  },
